\f1\fs22 The first thing Chanta explained to Hayley was the source of the flowers, the leftover muffin baskets on the table, and the impromptu warehouse of boxed sex toys behind the parlour couch, while Alexander sat, in a cushy single-seater with two Elvi looming behind him while Hayley eyed him like a sex predator. Apparently, he truly believed that obsessive and questionably appropriate gifting cast him a sincere romantic, not a deranged stalker.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kathy tended to Sarah upstairs, while Chanta filled Hayley in, oversharing in the way only a genuine innocent does when desperate to shake the law\'92s scrutiny from his back. \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Did you take any documents from the office where Travis Teh died?\'94 Hayley asked.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93No, I never took any documents,\'94 Chanta replied. Hayley sensed a gritty mote of a lie in what sounded fundamentally true.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Did you take anything at all?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93The only things I took were exploded body parts, and my own cleaning equipment and supplies.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>That rang truer, but Hayley still didn\'92t like it. \'93Then why does Agent Jones believe you did?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I honestly have no idea, detective.\'94  \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>For Chanta\'92s part, he knew Agent Jones was looking for that accursed disc which he guessed Sarah still possessed. He could truthfully state he hadn\'92t stolen documents or anything else from the suicide scene.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93He\'92s very convinced you did. He broke into your apartment and attacked you. Somehow, you and Sarah escaped.\'94  \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Alexander moaned in the background; Hayley pressed on. \'93He chased you to the Memphis Mafia Bar, where you escaped again, this time with the resolute help of the Elvi. And now you\'92re both ensconced in this mansion, defended by tanks and apparently revered as royalty.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93All I know, detective, is they like the lyrics I write, and their songs are now very popular.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93They are indeed,\'94 Hayley admitted. \'93Mr Chantara, are you the father of Sarah Jacobson\'92s baby?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93No!\'94 both Chanta and Alexander protested. As Alexander sat up, Chanta muttered bitterly, \'93She\'92s much too good for me.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>After the interview, Hayley thanked Chanta and dismissed him. She\'92d call for Sarah soon, but Alexander still needed handling.  \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Satisfied?\'94 she asked him.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Alexander crossed his legs and traced a finger down his top lip to the corner of his mouth. He raised it as if to make a point, then hesitated, mouth half open, finger wilting, while he rewrote the script in his head. His eyebrows grew decisive; his finger reasserted. \'93I owe you an apology, detective\'85 inspector.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Hayley savoured the moment, but remained wary.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I\'92d be happy to have contracts drawn up today to secure the rights to these story arcs you\'92ve devised.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Story arcs?\'94 Hayley waved the notion away and flicked it out the window. \'93It\'92s the simple truth.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93You are so passionately dedicated to your craft, detective inspector, and I respect that. I once believed the entertainment industry would crush your idealism, but I see now you possess a genuine talent. Let me just say\'97if I may, and please understand, I am completely sincere\'85 completely sincere, in a way I usually reserve for my own peerless work\'85 oh, look, shall I just come out and say it?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Please do.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93You are a genius, Detective Inspector Hayley Lanyards, a genius!\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93A genius\'85\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Own it, Hayley-kins. No need for humility here. My ego can take the hit.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Well, anytime your ego needs to take a hit, let me know.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Again with the charm, Hayley-puss. I never knew self-deprecation could be so delightful. I wonder if I can work it into my own schtick\'85\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Why mess with your brand?\'94 Hayley said. \'93It works.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Alexander\'92s eyes lit up; he chuckled. \'93Ha! You\'92re right, of course. But look, I must thank you\'97humbly, so far as my upbringing allows\'97for what you\'92ve done here.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93And what\'92s that?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93You\'92ve lifted the story, Holly-girl! Here I was scheming a tawdry little terrorist-kidnapper drama, but you\'92ve transcended all that! Transcended! The scale seeps all the way into the darkest halls of power, into the deep state of the nation, and shines sunlight on the corruption and backroom clock-tampering of the most secretive agency in the country.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93The Global Stasis Technicians Association?\'94 Hayley asked, only half playing.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Alexander laughed. \'93Fridge Force? Very funny, but no\'85 well, maybe\'85 no\'85 we\'92ll work them in for season two. Let\'92s focus on what\'92s got me all jolly about this collaboration, Helly-Whiskers. It\'92s a story of how TRAMPS\'97our very own police department\'97exposes a PAPER plot to rig the country by scaring the populace with fake terrorist threats and framing innocent nobodies. And we\'92ll be the ones to pull the curtain back! Uncover it all!\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Well, that\'92s kinda where I was headed all along.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I see that now! You\'92re a narrative genius, Detective Inspector Executive Producer Holy Lanyards.\'94 Alexander was too busy constructing his own grandiosity to notice Hayley lapsing into autopilot. Then he sobered as a new idea struck him. \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable> \'93I\'92m glad you got Chanta off the hook. He\'92s really a very good friend.\'94
